projects.description:
-	Conduct a comprehensive analysis to identify the explicit and implicit causes of challenges in the transfer process; 
-	Conduct a series of training courses in the process of transferring responsibility for managing the healthcare system in temporary detention facilities to the Ministry of Health. 
-	Develop and pilot a curriculum for medical personnel on identification and documentation of torture and ill-treatment.

In 2026 the focus will be on:
•	Supporting integration of SRHR-inputs within 3 joint 2025 UNCT reports (UNCT Report to CESCR; UNCT Report to CEDAW;  UNCT Report to UPR)
•	Supporting integration of SRHR provisions into the draft National Program for Social Inclusion of People with Disabilities 2026-2030, revised and updated by the Ministry of Labor and Social Protection with UN Agencies’ support;
•	Supporting integration of SRHR-inputs into the 2026 VNR Report;
•	Supporting final review, updating and re-validation of the National Programme on SRHR, including the revision of the costing for the updated Action Plan of the NPSRHR for the 2026–2028 period.  In 2026 the focus will be also on advocacy effort,  advocating for approval of the newly revised and validated costed National Programme on Sexual and Reproductive Health 2026 - 2028. The approval of the new National Program on Sexual and Reproductive Health and Rights is of the utmost importance, as it provides the foundational framework for international partners to align their support with the Ministry's efforts to further strengthen the capacity of national healthcare facilities to respond to the SRH care needs of the population through the provision of quality, life-saving, essential sexual and reproductive health services.

 •	Conduct capacity building for public servants on HRBA;
•	Develop and pilot training programme on HRBA to public servants;
•	Conduct capacity building and provide expert guidance to social field professionals, including in the field of support and care, to apply a human rights-based approach principles in their work
- Training for public servants on HRBA
